Thanks Havard, after rereading the docs on replication I picked that up and it gave me the desired results. My problem was reading c(y1,y2,y3) as cbind(y1,y2,y3) when first going through the docs, so I mistakenly thought that replicate was used when there were multiple outcomes per observation. I didn't notice replicate or copy used in any of the Volume I or II examples, so could I suggest that an example of each would be helpful? Now that I've seen a use case I understand that they're extraordinarily useful.

